            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s **Perseverance rover has completed a “marathon” on Mars**, reaching **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on the Red Planet. It hit the milestone on its **1,890th Martian day, or sol**, after about **five years and four months** of surface operations—less than half the time it took Opportunity to cover the same distance.【5:0†source】\n\nNASA also released an orbital image from the **Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter’s HiRISE camera**, taken June 13, 2026, showing Perseverance as a small speck with its long trail of wheel tracks. At that time, the rover was operating west of **Jezero Crater** in a region nicknamed **“Arbot.”**【5:0†source】\n\nAnother recent major development: Perseverance completed the **first AI-planned drives on another world**. NASA said the demonstration used a vision-capable generative AI system to create safe waypoints for the rover, reducing the need for human route planners in some navigation tasks.【5:2†source】\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, driving beyond Jezero Crater, studying Martian geology, and demonstrating new autonomous technologies while continuing its core mission of collecting samples and searching for signs of ancient habitability.",
